WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.605 --> 00:00:05.841
In the last section,

00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:10.040
we saw that the motif enumeration
algorithm that we proposed had a flaw:

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.920
It assumed that the motif
that we are looking for

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.510
must occur in every string in the
collection of strings under consideration,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.440
which is not necessarily true
in biological applications.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.842
As an alternative, our plan is to assign
a score to a given collection of k-mers

00:00:27.842 --> 00:00:30.350
chosen from the strings.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.420
So, say that we have collected
such a set of k-mers,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.740
which we call Motifs,
represented here as a matrix.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.140
We indicate the most frequent nucleotide
in each column of this matrix

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.440
by a capital letter,
breaking ties arbitrarily.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.530
So note that positions two and three in
the motif matrix are the most conserved.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.260
Nucleotide G occurs in these
positions in every k-mer,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.670
whereas position ten, at the end of
the motif matrix, is the least conserved.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:05.960
A common method of visualizing a motif
matrix is with a motif logo, in which

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.460
the size of the nucleotide correlates with
how often it occurs in the motif matrix.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.430
Taking the capital letter in each column,
construct a consensus string for

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:20.520
this particular choice of k-mers that will
also provide us with a candidate motif.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.851
Our goal then is to choose k-mers
from the underlying strings, so

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:29.420
that they produce the most
conserved motif matrix.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.440
Then, simply take the consensus string
as our desired biological motif.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.350
We therefore are going to define
the score of the motif matrix

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.520
as simply the number of
lowercase letters in the matrix.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.450
Counting the number of lowercase
letters column by column,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.854
we see that the score for
this choice of motifs is equal to 30.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.290
We can now restate our goal as
choosing a collection of k-mers

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.610
from the underlying strings in
order to minimize this score.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.310
So we now have a more appropriate
computational problem,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.190
which we call the Motif Finding Problem.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.510
A brute force algorithm for
the Motif Finding Problem would simply

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.650
consider every possible choice of
k-mers to form a motif matrix and

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.000
then simply take a collection of
k-mers having the lowest score.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.540
But, as with any brute force approach,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.609
we need to ask ourselves
how fast this algorithm is.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.040
Assuming that there are, say, t strings
under consideration, all of length n,

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.398
then there are (n-k+1)^t
such possibilities for

00:02:36.398 --> 00:02:38.080
how to form a motif matrix.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.480
Then, scoring the matrix
requires k*t steps.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:48.630
So, assuming that k is much smaller
than n, as is the case in practice,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:56.480
the overall running time of this approach
is approximately O(n^t * k*t).

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.360
Now, the n^t term means
that this algorithm is useless for

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.170
practical values of n and t, and so
we're going to need a faster algorithm.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.870
So, to improve on our existing
brute force approach,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.640
we will rethink the motif finding problem.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.950
Specifically, I've noted here that,
when we choose the motif matrix,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.070
it automatically gives
us a consensus string.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.050
Our new approach will be to choose
the consensus string first,

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.330
then look for a motif matrix that
scores best against this consensus.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.180
How do we achieve this?

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.930
Well, until now, we have been scoring
the motif matrix column-by-column.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.050
However, we could just as easily compute
the number of lower case symbols on

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.040
the motif matrix row-by-row.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:50.640
I want you to note that the number
of lower case symbols in a row

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.840
is equal to the number of symbols in
that row that don't match the consensus,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.810
which we know as the Hamming Distance
between that row and the consensus string.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.910
As a result, the score of
the motifs matrix is just the sum

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.080
Hamming Distances between each string
in the matrix and the consensus string,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:14.890
which we're going to write as
d(Consensus(Motifs), Motifs).

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.440
This discussion now leads us to
the equivalent Motif Finding Problem,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.875
in which we are looking for a pattern
that will serve as our consensus string,

00:04:23.875 --> 00:04:27.710
i.e., a pattern that minimizes
the distance to motifs

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.500
over all choices of motifs
in the underlying strings.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.280
It may still seem that we have not
made our work any easier though,

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.480
since we are now looking for a pattern
in addition to a collection of motifs,

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.579
whereas before we just looked for
a collection of motifs.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.113
However, we will see in the next section
that we don't need to search through every

00:04:49.113 --> 00:04:53.064
possible choice of motifs in order
to find an optimal consensus string,

00:04:53.064 --> 00:04:54.962
which is what we're looking for.

